\details{
\code{data} is the output of \code{\link[=kb_predict_biomass_survey]{kb_predict_biomass_survey()}} with its defaults: wet
biomass per m² of each in situ survey, with the survey's drone cover on its
row. Each survey with drone cover is one observation; surveys without it
are not fitted, and a message gives their number. The calibration takes each
survey's biomass per m² as that of its plot.

A survey's mean wet biomass (kg/m²) is modelled with a Normal likelihood
censored below at zero, so a survey with no plants counted enters as the
probability of zero or less. Its mean is \code{mu = biomass_floor + cover_slope * exp(site_effect + year_effect) * cover}: \code{cover_slope} is the wet biomass per
m² of canopy at a typical site and year, and \code{biomass_floor}, common to all
sites and years, the wet biomass of a plot with no delineated canopy. Its SD
is \code{sqrt(sd_residual^2 + (cv_residual * mu)^2 + biomass_sd^2)}, where
\code{biomass_sd} is the SD of the survey's biomass draws, from plant weight.

\code{cover} is \code{canopy_area_m2 / (1 - tide_height_slope * tide_height_m)} over
the plot area, capped at 1 (Timmer et al. 2024); a survey with canopy at a
tide height at or above \code{1 / tide_height_slope} is fully covered, and warns
when that holds on more than 5\% of the prior. The default prior on
\code{tide_height_slope} largely sets the correction: raise its mean for beds in
strong current. Flights at 2 m or below keep the correction within the tides
the prior was estimated at. Pass canopy already corrected for tide with
\code{tide_height_m = 0}.
}
